CAT = "fladjConcepts"; SEG = "CON"
Q = [
("A Florida homeowners policy has been in force for 40 days. To cancel it for a reason other than nonpayment, the insurer must give at least:",
 "20 days' notice", ["10 days' notice", "45 days' notice", "120 days' notice"],
 "s. 627.4133(2)(b) allows cancellation of a residential property policy in force 90 days or less, for reasons other than nonpayment, with at least 20 days' written notice; nonpayment takes 10 days and longer-running policies 120 days."),
("Because a property policy is a personal contract, the insured generally may not:",
 "Assign it without the insurer's OK", ["Cancel it before the policy expires", "Ask for an endorsement", "Add a mortgagee later"],
 "The insurer chose to insure a particular person, not just the property, so a policy cannot be assigned before a loss without the insurer's written consent."),
("An insurance contract is called aleatory because:",
 "The amounts exchanged can be unequal", ["Only one party makes a binding promise", "One party writes every term", "It must be put in writing"],
 "In an aleatory contract the values exchanged depend on chance: a small premium may buy a large payment, or no payment at all. A promise by one party only is a unilateral contract."),
("An adjuster notes that a homeowner has filed three small water claims in two years. This describes the homeowner's loss:",
 "Frequency", ["Severity", "Exposure", "Retention"],
 "Frequency is how often losses happen; severity is how large they are. Underwriters and adjusters watch both."),
("Under Florida law, a claim for more damage from the same peril the insurer has already adjusted is a:",
 "Supplemental claim", ["Reopened claim", "New initial claim", "Subrogation claim"],
 "s. 627.70132(1) defines a supplemental claim as one for additional loss from the same peril already adjusted; a reopened claim is a closed claim reopened for more costs on damage already disclosed."),
("Which of these risks is insurable?",
 "A house fire", ["A fall in share prices", "A losing lottery ticket", "A failed new business"],
 "Insurers cover pure risks, where the only outcomes are loss or no loss. Shares, lotteries and ventures are speculative risks that can also produce a gain."),
("In insurance, a hazard is a condition that:",
 "Raises the chance of loss", ["Directly causes the loss", "Measures the loss's size", "Moves risk to another party"],
 "A hazard, such as oily rags in a garage, increases the likelihood or severity of a loss. The peril is the cause itself, such as fire."),
("A Florida residential property insurer must give the policyholder the Homeowner Claims Bill of Rights within how long after the first communication about a claim?",
 "14 days", ["7 days", "30 days", "60 days"],
 "s. 627.7142 requires the Homeowner Claims Bill of Rights within 14 days after the insurer receives an initial communication about a claim; it summarizes existing Florida claim rights."),
("Which coverage departs from the principle of indemnity because it can pay more than the property's actual cash value?",
 "Replacement cost coverage", ["Actual cash value coverage", "A coinsurance clause", "Pro rata other insurance"],
 "Replacement cost pays to replace with new without deduction for depreciation, which can leave the insured better off than before. ACV, coinsurance and pro rata clauses all keep payment at or below indemnity."),
("A reciprocal insurance exchange is managed for its subscribers by a(n):",
 "Attorney-in-fact", ["Board of stockholders", "Surplus lines broker", "Mutual holding company"],
 "In a reciprocal, subscribers insure one another, and an attorney-in-fact runs the exchange for them under a power of attorney."),
("A surplus lines insurer that writes a Florida risk through a surplus lines agent is:",
 "Eligible but not admitted", ["Admitted in Florida", "Owned by the state", "Licensed only for life lines"],
 "Surplus lines (nonadmitted) insurers hold no Florida certificate of authority but may write risks the admitted market will not, if they are on the eligible list."),
("An admitted insurer is one that:",
 "Holds a Florida certificate of authority", ["Writes only excess or very unusual risks", "Was organized under Florida law", "Is owned by its own policyholders"],
 "Admitted means authorized to do business in the state. An insurer organized in Florida is domestic; one owned by policyholders is a mutual."),
("Under estoppel, an insurer can be barred from denying coverage when the insured:",
 "Reasonably relied on its conduct", ["Paid one premium a week past due", "Had a loss over the limit", "Renewed the policy twice"],
 "Estoppel stops a party from asserting a right when the other party relied, to its harm, on that party's words or conduct. Waiver is the voluntary giving up of a known right."),
("A policy's liberalization clause means a broader form approved during the policy term:",
 "Applies to the current policy", ["Applies only at renewal", "Needs a new application", "Raises the premium straight away"],
 "If the insurer adopts a broader version of the form with no extra premium during the term, the liberalization condition extends that broader coverage to the existing policy."),
("Under Florida's valued policy law, when a building's total loss is caused partly by a covered peril and partly by an uncovered one, the insurer owes:",
 "Only the loss from the covered peril", ["The full face amount of the policy", "Nothing at all for the building", "Half of the face amount"],
 "s. 627.702(1)(b) pays the full policy amount only when a covered peril causes the total loss; with mixed causes, such as hurricane wind and storm surge, the insurer pays the part caused by the covered peril."),
("Vicarious liability holds a person responsible for:",
 "Another person's negligence", ["Harm from blasting work", "A breach of written contract", "A loss that no one caused"],
 "Vicarious liability is liability imputed from another, such as an employer for an employee acting in the course of work. Blasting is the classic case of strict (absolute) liability."),
]
